    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0135",
      "sequence": 147,
      "meaning": "Wherever Nārada sits preening, the bewildered princess refuses even to look. He repeatedly cranes forward in agitation while Śiva’s attendants smile at his plight. The compassionate Lord appears in royal form, and the delighted princess places the garland on him; Lakṣmī’s Lord departs with his bride, leaving all the kings disappointed. Nārada is distraught and bewildered, as though a jewel had fallen from an untied knot. The attendants advise him to look at his face in a mirror, then flee in fear. Seeing his face reflected in water, Nārada’s anger surges and he curses both tricksters to become deceitful, sinful night-rangers and reap the fruit of mocking a sage.",
